 ('Radio notes', '''<h2>ICOM</h2><p>Added in Ver0.77: IC-7200 / IC-7410 / IC-7600 / IC-9100 (documentation reviewed, hardware not tested). Select USB audio input and output separately. Set DATA modulation input to USB on the radio. IC-7600 uses D1, so set DATA1 input to USB. On IC-9100, select the operating MAIN side on the radio. Change filters on the radio for these four models.</p>
 <p>Match the radio CI-V address. Manual settings are retained, including an IC-7300MK2 operated with the IC-7300 address.</p>
-<h2>Yaesu / Kenwood (Hamlib)</h2><p>Supported selections include Yaesu FT-991/A, FTX-1, FT-710, FTDX10, FTDX101D/MP, FTDX3000 and Kenwood TS-590SG, TS-890S, TS-990S. About PSRTTY shows the Hamlib version and license. Specify CAT port and speed, then test the connection. For Yaesu, use Enhanced COM, USB DATA input and CAT RTS DISABLE (OFF). FTX-1 requires firmware supporting CAT.</p>
+<h2>Yaesu / Kenwood (Hamlib)</h2><p>Supported selections include Yaesu FT-817 (FT-817ND), FT-818ND, FT-991/A, FTX-1, FT-710, FTDX10, FTDX101D/MP, FTDX3000 and Kenwood TS-590SG, TS-890S, TS-990S. About PSRTTY shows the Hamlib version and license. Specify CAT port and speed, then test the connection. For Yaesu, use Enhanced COM, USB DATA input and CAT RTS DISABLE (OFF). FTX-1 requires firmware supporting CAT.</p>
 <h3>Mode selection</h3><p>Select the radio mode above Antenna TUNE in Control. ICOM offers LSB-D / USB-D / LSB / USB; Yaesu shows model-specific DATA modes; Kenwood shows LSB-DATA / USB-DATA / LSB / USB. TS-990S also offers D1–D3. Modes cannot change during TX. Automatic DATA selection runs only on connection; temporary changes here are not saved.</p>
 <h3>Connection test</h3><p>A successful test connection ends after about 10 seconds. Press Connect for continued operation. If a test tone is transmitting, the test connection waits until transmission ends.</p>
 <h3>Connection button</h3><p>Press Disconnected at the upper left to connect, or the blue Connect button to disconnect. Repeated clicks are blocked while connecting. Hover to see the model and COM port.</p>
@@ -94,4 +94,3 @@ PAGES_EN[-1]=(PAGES_EN[-1][0], PAGES_EN[-1][1]+'<h2>Hide short received messages
 PAGES_EN.append(('HAMLOG / integrations', '<h2>HAMLOG and other integrations</h2>\n<h3>HAMLOG target: Ver5.48</h3><p>Uses the Turbo HAMLOG/Win public API introduced in Ver5.27c. On Windows, start one HAMLOG instance and open its QSO input window. Run both programs with the same privilege level.</p>\n<ol><li>Open <b>Integration → HAMLOG integration settings</b>. Check the connection; the responding window title is displayed.</li><li>Enable transfer when logging. This defaults to OFF. The default save mode fills HAMLOG input fields only; save the QSO in HAMLOG yourself. Alternatively, choose to request saving after transfer. Save these settings; they are shared by all profiles.</li><li>After PSRTTY saves a QSO to ADIF, it transfers it to HAMLOG. Manual logging and TU73 auto logging use the same path. PSRTTY increments the sent serial only once.</li></ol>\n<p>CALL, JST date/time, sent/received RST, frequency and RTTY mode are transferred. SENT and RCVD are retained in Remarks1; the station CALL is in Remarks2. Contest exchanges are not inferred as JCC/JCG codes. HAMLOG QSL, Code, GL, Name and QTH settings/lookup results are retained.</p>\n<h3>CALL / Name / QTH lookup</h3><p>Lookup in the settings window enters a CALL in HAMLOG’s empty input window, invokes HAMLOG’s Enter processing and displays Name/QTH. Results depend on HAMLOG lookup settings and stored records and may be empty. Logging that same CALL fills the draft created by lookup. If the draft was edited in HAMLOG, automatic transfer is stopped to protect it. Save or clear the draft before proceeding.</p>\n<h3>Saving and transfer failures</h3><p>In input-only mode, save the QSO in HAMLOG before recording the next QSO. Other QSO drafts are never overwritten. Automatic saving reads back and checks the transfer before invoking HAMLOG’s Save API. You can check the result in HAMLOG’s input window and log.</p><p>Failures show a dialog with the reason and suggested action. The PSRTTY ADIF record is already saved: do not add that QSO again. Interrupted transfers are journaled and not retried automatically. Check HAMLOG and manually enter only missing QSOs, or use File → HAMLOG CSV export. Records, edits and deletions in HAMLOG do not synchronize back to PSRTTY.</p>\n<h3>zLog target: 3.0.4.0 (ZLOG3040)</h3><p>Integration → zLog Reiwa integration settings opens ADIF export. Selected QSOs are transferred by file, without live radio/log control. File → ADIF export opens the same export wizard.</p><ol><li>In zLog, select the matching contest, station CALL and time settings. ADIF times are UTC; zLog converts them according to its contest settings.</li><li>Select the export format, period, station CALL, bands and QSOs, then save.</li><li>Import the ADIF file in zLog and check count, time, band, mode, both RSTs, both exchanges and multipliers. Avoid importing the same file twice.</li></ol>\n<table><tr><th>Format</th><th>Exchange mapping</th></tr><tr><td>Standard ADIF</td><td>STX_STRING/SRX_STRING and numeric STX/SRX, as before.</td></tr><tr><td>General zLog</td><td>Also retains original SENT/RCVD and station CALL in COMMENT.</td></tr><tr><td>JARL WW RTTY</td><td>Received age also goes into AGE. Enter one or two digits.</td></tr><tr><td>CQ WW RTTY</td><td>Received zone goes into CQZ and state/province into STATE, e.g. 05 MA. Zone must be 01–40.</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>The target zLog ADIF importer does not include state/province in the CQ WW RTTY received exchange.</b>After import, use the original RCVD in the note to correct affected exchanges and multipliers. Numeric fields may lose leading zeroes in their display; the original text is retained in string fields and notes. Check contest configuration and exchanges before submitting a contest log.</p>\n<h3>Radio controls</h3><p>NB and NR read/write are enabled through Hamlib for all listed Yaesu/Kenwood models, with state readback. Yaesu uses the DNR label. FT-991/FT-991A, FTX-1, FT-710, FTDX10, FTDX101D/MP and FTDX3000 offer mode-specific receive widths. TS-590SG DATA/SSB width is adjusted by changing high cut while preserving low cut.</p><p>Hamlib 4.7.2 does not implement receive bandwidth control for TS-890S/TS-990S; use the radio controls for width. NB/NR remain available. Controls are disabled during TX or when connection/state retrieval fails.</p>'))
 
 PAGES_EN[0] = (PAGES_EN[0][0], PAGES_EN[0][1] + '\n<h3>IC-7760 (Ver1.08)</h3><p>Default CI-V address: B2. Select the USB CI-V COM port and set DATA1 modulation input to USB on the radio. LSB-D/USB-D selects DATA1. Controls apply to the selected receiver; check the transmit side on the radio.</p>\n<h3>Secondary Audio OUT</h3><p>Open Secondary Audio OUT settings to the left of Save on the Audio OUT tab. Confirm and save the primary device and volume before opening. Cancel or save failure leaves the window closed. The primary device, including Auto, is excluded from the secondary list. Enable it (default OFF), select a different device from the primary output, choose volume and Save. Close discards unsaved changes. Settings are stored per profile.</p><p>The same TX waveform is sent for monitoring or other uses, with independent volume from 0–200% (default 100%). Above 100% the slider turns red and audio may distort. This window has no PTT or test controls. Stopping TX also stops the secondary output. While disconnected, enabling both disconnected TX and secondary Audio OUT allows playback through either available output. If neither works, a reason is displayed. Connected radio transmission still requires the primary output. On errors, check the selected device and connection as indicated in the status area.</p>')
